nts are $1,$ $f,$ $2,$ and $g,$ and they are listed between two braces." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-841027ba-dd4e-5448-93c3-df146c3ec885"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-841027ba-dd4e-5448-93c3-df146c3ec885" :answer-field/id #uuid "841027ba-dd4e-5448-93c3-df146c3ec885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff43a729-f5c5-5a8e-a699-84816fa6c4a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0127109a-3730-548f-bf3d-07a26a3905f6" "answer-f47e1594-3e4e-52fc-b3b4-036ed29b53bf" "answer-e1204290-3544-5af4-bfb1-86ea28a53325" "answer-5bb2439b-f439-585e-abab-de3ea1a458a0" "answer-ff43a729-f5c5-5a8e-a699-84816fa6c4a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0127109a-3730-548f-bf3d-07a26a3905f6" :answer/id #uuid "0127109a-3730-548f-bf3d-07a26a3905f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,f,2,g\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f47e1594-3e4e-52fc-b3b4-036ed29b53bf" :answer/id #uuid "f47e1594-3e4e-52fc-b3b4-036ed29b53bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1204290-3544-5af4-bfb1-86ea28a53325" :answer/id #uuid "e1204290-3544-5af4-bfb1-86ea28a53325" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{f,\\{h\\}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bb2439b-f439-585e-abab-de3ea1a458a0" :answer/id #uuid "5bb2439b-f439-585e-abab-de3ea1a458a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{∅,\\{f,g\\}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff43a729-f5c5-5a8e-a699-84816fa6c4a3" :answer/id #uuid "ff43a729-f5c5-5a8e-a699-84816fa6c4a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f,g,h" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054972 :question/problem "If $a,b,$ and $c$ are elements of $S$, but $d$ and $e$ are not, then which of the following is true?" :question/worked-solution "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $a,b,$ and $c$ are elements of $S,$ we write $a,b,c\\in S.$\n\n- Since $d$ and $e$ are *not* elements of $S,$ we write $d,e\\notin S.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $a,b,c\\in S$ and $d,e\\notin S$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051362 :answer-field/correct "answer-5b38006e-2ad1-52eb-ad48-c5a73b96e9b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8b84e9a-76dd-5810-b594-e0762d48398c" "answer-3c63e39c-ecfa-5d5a-9f4f-bd3b390019cd" "answer-5b38006e-2ad1-52eb-ad48-c5a73b96e9b4" "answer-de04cc81-7aaa-50c1-a197-5a3b9ca7cbe4" "answer-e95b841b-9de7-591f-9b54-0662ea51e111"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8b84e9a-76dd-5810-b594-e0762d48398c" :answer/id #uuid "b8b84e9a-76dd-5810-b594-e0762d48398c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a,b,c\\notin S" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c63e39c-ecfa-5d5a-9f4f-bd3b390019cd" :answer/id #uuid "3c63e39c-ecfa-5d5a-9f4f-bd3b390019cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d,e\\in S" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b38006e-2ad1-52eb-ad48-c5a73b96e9b4" :answer/id #uuid "5b38006e-2ad1-52eb-ad48-c5a73b96e9b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a,b,c\\in S$ and $d,e\\notin S$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de04cc81-7aaa-50c1-a197-5a3b9ca7cbe4" :answer/id #uuid "de04cc81-7aaa-50c1-a197-5a3b9ca7cbe4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a,b,c\\notin S$ and $d,e\\in S$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e95b841b-9de7-591f-9b54-0662ea51e111" :answer/id #uuid "e95b841b-9de7-591f-9b54-0662ea51e111" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a,b\\in S$ and $c,d,e\\notin S$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ae10c4a-b478-5b9e-9599-7eb7b75dca9d" :question/id #uuid "6ae10c4a-b478-5b9e-9599-7eb7b75dca9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101933" :question/problem "If ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B$, but ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are not, then which of the following is true?" :question/worked-solution "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B,$ we write ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B.$\n\n- Since ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B$ and ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-666a3742-fc17-5528-9ff4-7003969fc507"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-666a3742-fc17-5528-9ff4-7003969fc507" :answer-field/id #uuid "666a3742-fc17-5528-9ff4-7003969fc507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-64079805-e640-5e9e-b17b-eef19f4b9ee0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83e1229a-7aa6-5f6e-abda-ca318568130c" "answer-430f142b-079d-5d36-b9ed-c7fcb1a257fc" "answer-64079805-e640-5e9e-b17b-eef19f4b9ee0" "answer-6e9a1d45-8d06-5fa1-807a-558577aa972d" "answer-0447a795-c6d5-5dfd-ada1-ec30242c71e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e1229a-7aa6-5f6e-abda-ca318568130c" :answer/id #uuid "83e1229a-7aa6-5f6e-abda-ca318568130c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\notin B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-430f142b-079d-5d36-b9ed-c7fcb1a257fc" :answer/id #uuid "430f142b-079d-5d36-b9ed-c7fcb1a257fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\in B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64079805-e640-5e9e-b17b-eef19f4b9ee0" :answer/id #uuid "64079805-e640-5e9e-b17b-eef19f4b9ee0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B$ and ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e9a1d45-8d06-5fa1-807a-558577aa972d" :answer/id #uuid "6e9a1d45-8d06-5fa1-807a-558577aa972d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\in B$ and ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\notin B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0447a795-c6d5-5dfd-ada1-ec30242c71e3" :answer/id #uuid "0447a795-c6d5-5dfd-ada1-ec30242c71e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${3}^{4}\\in B$ and ${2}^{5},{2}^{7}\\notin B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b96bbb9-30d0-511e-a26c-5b394ffc1b3c" :question/id #uuid "6b96bbb9-30d0-511e-a26c-5b394ffc1b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101922" :question/problem "If $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B$, but $v$ and $w$ are not, then which of the following is true?" :question/worked-solution "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B,$ we write $x,y,z\\in B.$\n\n- Since $v$ and $w$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write $v,w\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $x,y,z\\in B$ and $v,w\\notin B$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83ddee6b-0ee6-53e7-9738-f66e538f4e76"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83ddee6b-0ee6-53e7-9738-f66e538f4e76" :answer-field/id #uuid "83ddee6b-0ee6-53e7-9738-f66e538f4e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c2bc8fd-0245-51f5-9eb9-80380f45f77c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0be63167-6f24-5aa9-82e2-881c75a4fcf6" "answer-5be50ca2-0ec1-5976-816f-395c808ddc72" "answer-1c2bc8fd-0245-51f5-9eb9-80380f45f77c" "answer-11399e5f-3461-57e4-bb07-738143f0cb77" "answer-dc2f42fb-f6fb-5af0-bf6c-4e468f6a011c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0be63167-6f24-5aa9-82e2-881c75a4fcf6" :answer/id #uuid "0be63167-6f24-5aa9-82e2-881c75a4fcf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v,w\\in B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5be50ca2-0ec1-5976-816f-395c808ddc72" :answer/id #uuid "5be50ca2-0ec1-5976-816f-395c808ddc72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x,y,z\\notin B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c2bc8fd-0245-51f5-9eb9-80380f45f77c" :answer/id #uuid "1c2bc8fd-0245-51f5-9eb9-80380f45f77c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x,y,z\\in B$ and $v,w\\notin B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11399e5f-3461-57e4-bb07-738143f0cb77" :answer/id #uuid "11399e5f-3461-57e4-bb07-738143f0cb77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x,y,z\\notin B$ and $v,w\\in B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc2f42fb-f6fb-5af0-bf6c-4e468f6a011c" :answer/id #uuid "dc2f42fb-f6fb-5af0-bf6c-4e468f6a011c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x,y\\in B$ and $z,v,w\\notin B$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f10fdf14-c3b0-5ff7-a1e1-67a02a7a3852" :question/id #uuid "f10fdf14-c3b0-5ff7-a1e1-67a02a7a3852" :question/math-academy-id "q-80286" :question/problem "If $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y$, but $0$ and $1$ are not, then which of the following is true?" :question/worked-solution "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y,$ we write $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y.$\n\n- Since $0$ and $1$ are *not* elements of $Y,$ we write $0,1\\notin Y.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y$ and $0,1\\notin Y$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-54aa798f-1fb7-558c-8c8b-32e0cf00221c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54aa798f-1fb7-558c-8c8b-32e0cf00221c" :answer-field/id #uuid "54aa798f-1fb7-558c-8c8b-32e0cf00221c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f202e225-7c13-544d-919d-7cf5555f73f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6fdb368-49f9-53cd-8d4a-966db82c4693" "answer-a7a22407-4ed6-5a83-8beb-894b416066bd" "answer-15fd2bc1-c68a-55a5-bd2a-a662816264a4" "answer-f202e225-7c13-544d-919d-7cf5555f73f5" "answer-fc797368-1b18-5f08-84b5-498afafdbc38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6fdb368-49f9-53cd-8d4a-966db82c4693" :answer/id #uuid "c6fdb368-49f9-53cd-8d4a-966db82c4693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,1,\\pi ,\\text{e}\\in Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7a22407-4ed6-5a83-8beb-894b416066bd" :answer/id #uuid "a7a22407-4ed6-5a83-8beb-894b416066bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1,\\pi ,\\sqrt{2}\\notin Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15fd2bc1-c68a-55a5-bd2a-a662816264a4" :answer/id #uuid "15fd2bc1-c68a-55a5-bd2a-a662816264a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0\\in Y$ and $\\pi ,\\sqrt{2}\\notin Y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f202e225-7c13-544d-919d-7cf5555f73f5" :answer/id #uuid "f202e225-7c13-544d-919d-7cf5555f73f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y$ and $0,1\\notin Y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc797368-1b18-5f08-84b5-498afafdbc38" :answer/id #uuid "fc797368-1b18-5f08-84b5-498afafdbc38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\notin Y$ and $0,1\\in Y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-101cf0dd-9e7d-5358-b3ec-977545014b49" :question/id #uuid "101cf0dd-9e7d-5358-b3ec-977545014b49" :question/math-academy-id "q-80294" :question/problem "Which set represents all positive integers less than or equal to $15?$" :question/worked-solution "To express the desired set, we start with the first positive integer less than or equal to $15,$ which is $1.$ The next positive integer is $2,$ the one after that is $3,$ and so on up to $15$ (which is the last number that is less than or equal to $15$).\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\{1,2,3,…,15\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42187262-f8cb-5e17-9c88-a9b4b6fc1094"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42187262-f8cb-5e17-9c88-a9b4b6fc1094" :answer-field/id #uuid "42187262-f8cb-5e17-9c88-a9b4b6fc1094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-141ad6e6-6929-578e-bacc-9597f6a3ef43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-976b9ccb-f7eb-5fd8-aab8-ccbbc0ace82c" "answer-141ad6e6-6929-578e-bacc-9597f6a3ef43" "answer-0f39c390-6ae8-57ba-a0c8-09fbb39812dc" "answer-b21b2b22-ae8e-5dff-acd6-97c845f86570" "answer-06d8ffd2-50f9-5666-8b0a-9d861689836a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-976b9ccb-f7eb-5fd8-aab8-ccbbc0ace82c" :answer/id #uuid "976b9ccb-f7eb-5fd8-aab8-ccbbc0ace82c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,2,3,…,14\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-141ad6e6-6929-578e-bacc-9597f6a3ef43" :answer/id #uuid "141ad6e6-6929-578e-bacc-9597f6a3ef43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,2,3,…,15\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f39c390-6ae8-57ba-a0c8-09fbb39812dc" :answer/id #uuid "0f39c390-6ae8-57ba-a0c8-09fbb39812dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3,5,…,14\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b21b2b22-ae8e-5dff-acd6-97c845f86570" :answer/id #uuid "b21b2b22-ae8e-5dff-acd6-97c845f86570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{…,12,13,14\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06d8ffd2-50f9-5666-8b0a-9d861689836a" :answer/id #uuid "06d8ffd2-50f9-5666-8b0a-9d861689836a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{…,13,14,15\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-76066cd5-d606-50ce-8894-909f30c9f6a3" :question/id #uuid "76066cd5-d606-50ce-8894-909f30c9f6a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104952" :question/problem "Which set represents all integers divisible by $5$ from $-21$ to $11?$" :question/worked-solution "To express the desired set, we start with the first integer divisible by $5$ greater than or equal to $-21,$ which is $-20.$ The next integer divisible by $5$ is $-15,$ the one after that is $-10,$ and so on up to $10$ (which is the last number that is divisible by $5$ and less than or equal to $11$).\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{-20,-15,-10,…,10\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bf3c0e7-4219-5e25-bc7d-63783b1bd456"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bf3c0e7-4219-5e25-bc7d-63783b1bd456" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf3c0e7-4219-5e25-bc7d-63783b1bd456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2fcc56eb-0a58-5a35-8189-ba6da205b93b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ae35147-d8d9-5552-8ef0-e5921f034714" "answer-2fcc56eb-0a58-5a35-8189-ba6da205b93b" "answer-b9858cce-17aa-5336-a354-b3bcd71e3c17" "answer-1f52347f-2916-55e5-abc8-4a087e3c5c15" "answer-ae3c24a5-7306-5650-af15-bc07972ae031"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ae35147-d8d9-5552-8ef0-e5921f034714" :answer/id #uuid "1ae35147-d8d9-5552-8ef0-e5921f034714" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-20,-10,0,10\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fcc56eb-0a58-5a35-8189-ba6da205b93b" :answer/id #uuid "2fcc56eb-0a58-5a35-8189-ba6da205b93b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-20,-15,-10,…,10\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9858cce-17aa-5336-a354-b3bcd71e3c17" :answer/id #uuid "b9858cce-17aa-5336-a354-b3bcd71e3c17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-20,-15,-10,…,15\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f52347f-2916-55e5-abc8-4a087e3c5c15" :answer/id #uuid "1f52347f-2916-55e5-abc8-4a087e3c5c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-20,-15,-10,…,5\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3c24a5-7306-5650-af15-bc07972ae031" :answer/id #uuid "ae3c24a5-7306-5650-af15-bc07972ae031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-25,-20,-15,…,10\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056036 :tutorial/content "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{ \\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}.\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow},$ $\\text{red},$ and $\\text{blue}.$\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\quad  \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n\n$$\nT = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}\n$$\n\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nA = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}\n$$\n\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nB = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}\n$$\n\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056037 :tutorial/content "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.  \n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in,$ for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A,$ we write\n\n$$\na \\in A.\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{ \\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\},\n$$\n\nwe can write \n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A,$ then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A.\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056038 :tutorial/content "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly. \n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\". \n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n\n$$\nB = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ contains infinitely many elements:\n\n- A set containing a finite number of elements is called a **finite set**.\n\n- A set containing an infinite number of elements is called an **infinite set**.\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ are finite sets, while $C$ is an infinite set." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054904 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186181248 :answer-field/choices 17592186181249] [:db/retract 17592186181248 :answer-field/choices 17592186181250] [:db/retract 17592186181254 :answer-field/choices 17592186181256] [:db/retract 17592186181254 :answer-field/choices 17592186181258] [:db/retract 17592186183115 :answer-field/choices 17592186183120] [:db/retract 17592186183115 :answer-field/choices 17592186183116] [:db/retract 17592186183115 :answer-field/choices 17592186183117] [:db/retract 17592186183115 :answer-field/choices 17592186183118] [:db/retract 17592186183115 :answer-field/choices 17592186183119] {:db/id 17592186184344 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-81392296-c859-50aa-9dad-b2d6c26af157" "question-3eab3230-c0ce-5edb-86e7-28c4e5295d2f" "question-09d173fe-eece-5b8b-9031-7816fb223b07" "question-47ab5bde-d639-547f-ad10-9ccc080ffe38"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184045 :question/problem "![](images/31/31cc010ebe0a36d7c94ff0e5dd33c6c09a6d13c88f8c4696e32ce620dd037b00.png)\n\n \n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9d/9daed2ca60f9277d0b4b02c3c013446dc696960fc51065a83cd1c213a5afefde.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{a}$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184345 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e02af7d-5317-58cc-b4e8-ba89b18e708e" "question-b3de56e3-5772-5207-831f-4a8a7dca824e" "question-525ecf36-0f83-5c22-a5cd-0246fa04eb42" "question-a40aacc4-bbfa-50e9-8365-3467adc8fc1b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184046 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c625bd3abc4fd02cf26119315911b24d06f8b31aa2977cf1571a8741ce091dd.png)\n\n   \n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ as shown below. So, $\\mathbf{b}$ defines the direction of the first principal component.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4539ab239ecf0e54d5e828079469b928dd9201ae5aa913db13104bf1ed1796.png)\n\n   \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{c},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d1e84cc1a20da39af058b422ea7860a11eb97c0afa59be752b0ca3ed0a14be.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{c}$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dd2cd7be-06ce-5ffb-8860-cd5d1eb5c130" "question-68cd1b00-9920-5784-9707-6f682f05d636" "question-7eb658cd-5383-5f28-8b76-9d708b2f6422" "question-2381e2c6-90e5-5e9a-8022-1f6b70cb4895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184047 :question/problem "A geologist has measured two specific features, $x_1$ and $x_2,$ for each rock among a sample of rocks she is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular rock.\nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately \n![](images/5d/5d1c7f2402d1142e6d7ab3787a2459e19607a5aaf97761c3dff917a5a74deb42.svg)\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the geologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/97/97a46ed0c33579b24a5c43003b908ad7b04c78a3e5b5486d4bdc5d0fe2bb3bee.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n![](images/a8/a8013014e445cf1099127a588812b42312c7ce7d307b23a21b9b496509949e5a.svg)\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables $Y = XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation ![](images/88/888217508e2871244251b77c465094fa6e581ccc36b92aa1ebc62a98f8a25654.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.447 & -0.894 \\\\ 0.894 & -0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.447{x}_{1}+0.894{x}_{2} & -0.894{x}_{1}-0.447{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that $y_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-81392296-c859-50aa-9dad-b2d6c26af157" :question/id #uuid "81392296-c859-50aa-9dad-b2d6c26af157" :question/math-academy-id "q-148481" :question/problem "![](images/13/137e230421e20e7694526973fd4a92f9d573f1d97273151504df940b955d48f9.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5efefe99cb4fe0680d8ea3aaa31a05767f187e54acfb5b28145194e7dec24f3b.png)\n\n \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ and $c$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc0ba6fb-0342-54eb-8882-734aaf999dd0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc0ba6fb-0342-54eb-8882-734aaf999dd0" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0ba6fb-0342-54eb-8882-734aaf999dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6bb5a88c-74b4-5280-9f14-8e9e9c0c6430" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18dd9820-39c3-5233-ba6a-e12519fef3e3" "answer-452841d2-3309-51f2-a5c3-494e5fb7b675" "answer-6bb5a88c-74b4-5280-9f14-8e9e9c0c6430" "answer-517197fd-cd9b-5a0c-9035-00b5e36c9d64" "answer-41f18862-e3e4-5f8e-a2a1-87403cde14b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18dd9820-39c3-5233-ba6a-e12519fef3e3" :answer/id #uuid "18dd9820-39c3-5233-ba6a-e12519fef3e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a$  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-452841d2-3309-51f2-a5c3-494e5fb7b675" :answer/id #uuid "452841d2-3309-51f2-a5c3-494e5fb7b675" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a$ and $b$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/valida