e unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189676 :answer-field/correct "answer-d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1caf9374-a8b9-56f8-9a51-1f6c33377318" "answer-cf081a75-0c42-57a1-9dee-9c14a25e2410" "answer-478cdd6a-a11c-5ded-9658-0ec619567053" "answer-d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" "answer-b9f028a0-351b-53d6-95ad-6d6df0bd24cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1caf9374-a8b9-56f8-9a51-1f6c33377318" :answer/id #uuid "1caf9374-a8b9-56f8-9a51-1f6c33377318" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf081a75-0c42-57a1-9dee-9c14a25e2410" :answer/id #uuid "cf081a75-0c42-57a1-9dee-9c14a25e2410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-478cdd6a-a11c-5ded-9658-0ec619567053" :answer/id #uuid "478cdd6a-a11c-5ded-9658-0ec619567053" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer/id #uuid "d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9f028a0-351b-53d6-95ad-6d6df0bd24cf" :answer/id #uuid "b9f028a0-351b-53d6-95ad-6d6df0bd24cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ddc6be0-7f0e-552b-88a6-224cbf5ee88c" :question/id #uuid "5ddc6be0-7f0e-552b-88a6-224cbf5ee88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138521" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $4,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-283a2bc1-2dc2-54df-b080-f5e691fb529a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-283a2bc1-2dc2-54df-b080-f5e691fb529a" :answer-field/id #uuid "283a2bc1-2dc2-54df-b080-f5e691fb529a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89d016a3-becd-5d35-807d-bea843a21d96" "answer-d1b50652-4f11-5f47-9388-bfd66be24cb1" "answer-dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" "answer-9a2c0e02-bfc6-53c8-ba64-3ec942a9b78a" "answer-6efbdee7-7d3a-5707-9520-c57843d2fe06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89d016a3-becd-5d35-807d-bea843a21d96" :answer/id #uuid "89d016a3-becd-5d35-807d-bea843a21d96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1b50652-4f11-5f47-9388-bfd66be24cb1" :answer/id #uuid "d1b50652-4f11-5f47-9388-bfd66be24cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer/id #uuid "dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a2c0e02-bfc6-53c8-ba64-3ec942a9b78a" :answer/id #uuid "9a2c0e02-bfc6-53c8-ba64-3ec942a9b78a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6efbdee7-7d3a-5707-9520-c57843d2fe06" :answer/id #uuid "6efbdee7-7d3a-5707-9520-c57843d2fe06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1dfd23ff-dd1b-5e4d-b783-10b5e0f038d4" :question/id #uuid "1dfd23ff-dd1b-5e4d-b783-10b5e0f038d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149404" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $4,$ and $8.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-145dd816-7538-567d-8cf5-d59c4f788fe9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-145dd816-7538-567d-8cf5-d59c4f788fe9" :answer-field/id #uuid "145dd816-7538-567d-8cf5-d59c4f788fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ae9e2d9-02b8-5bf3-9e21-3b0ccae3f4f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a6b67ad-416f-59d6-a159-dc4add175bb2" "answer-30a3113b-fc17-596b-9f95-def6091c6999" "answer-aa0c7856-5a8b-5fd6-87db-61cf81268afc" "answer-5ae9e2d9-02b8-5bf3-9e21-3b0ccae3f4f6" "answer-d24bafaa-3b7e-53f5-9a86-3e1dc5279c5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a6b67ad-416f-59d6-a159-dc4add175bb2" :answer/id #uuid "7a6b67ad-416f-59d6-a159-dc4add175bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30a3113b-fc17-596b-9f95-def6091c6999" :answer/id #uuid "30a3113b-fc17-596b-9f95-def6091c6999" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa0c7856-5a8b-5fd6-87db-61cf81268afc" :answer/id #uuid "aa0c7856-5a8b-5fd6-87db-61cf81268afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ae9e2d9-02b8-5bf3-9e21-3b0ccae3f4f6" :answer/id #uuid "5ae9e2d9-02b8-5bf3-9e21-3b0ccae3f4f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d24bafaa-3b7e-53f5-9a86-3e1dc5279c5e" :answer/id #uuid "d24bafaa-3b7e-53f5-9a86-3e1dc5279c5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193823 :tutorial/content "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$ If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is \n\n![](images/e4/e40cd1a0df89ba171b921ab971e8eee25e284265df13b7e031ea32fc28d2c27b.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=10.$ \n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/c7/c7050bbb395b939b1b0559b3e7515c4be4f572aed61090f1a4489e126763240f.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/7b/7b0a41deb385aa2471cae701c9f87938085256879a8d834535f8ec0e3f431f27.svg) \n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is ![](images/94/9490f2e5105592fa3976ae506cbdf64d70c3372d1cc9295eaa6c6580a4ac75aa.svg)\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector ![](images/e2/e2b366cfb8cb5b708eb5ba443dfc61c05b498d4eb737b375c8ebc86b96e8eea6.svg) also works in the example above." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192865 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/id #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71405" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" "answer-3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" "answer-6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" "answer-92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" "answer-201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/id #uuid "45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/id #uuid "3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/id #uuid "6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/id #uuid "201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f14a258c-8230-5ba0-9c10-e3821900b845" :activity/id #uuid "f14a258c-8230-5ba0-9c10-e3821900b845" :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050027 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-7a1f6e0c-4f5f-50c7-b0d9-d452532aced4" "step-d0f5d1fa-3cf9-5f94-8815-62bf015679e5" "step-2c774462-5887-556e-8c1c-42d4884f6cfe" "step-f9ae40c4-6ef1-5389-b6b7-49816af3ef18" "step-a9a38a9f-1171-55bf-a06a-39b676731e34"] :activity/first-step "step-7a1f6e0c-4f5f-50c7-b0d9-d452532aced4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7a1f6e0c-4f5f-50c7-b0d9-d452532aced4" :step/id #uuid "7a1f6e0c-4f5f-50c7-b0d9-d452532aced4" :step/content 17592186125190 :step/next "step-d0f5d1fa-3cf9-5f94-8815-62bf015679e5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d0f5d1fa-3cf9-5f94-8815-62bf015679e5" :step/id #uuid "d0f5d1fa-3cf9-5f94-8815-62bf015679e5" :step/content 17592186338938 :step/next "step-2c774462-5887-556e-8c1c-42d4884f6cfe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c774462-5887-556e-8c1c-42d4884f6cfe" :step/id #uuid "2c774462-5887-556e-8c1c-42d4884f6cfe" :step/content "question-cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :step/next "step-f9ae40c4-6ef1-5389-b6b7-49816af3ef18" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f9ae40c4-6ef1-5389-b6b7-49816af3ef18" :step/id #uuid "f9ae40c4-6ef1-5389-b6b7-49816af3ef18" :step/content 17592186265960 :step/next "step-a9a38a9f-1171-55bf-a06a-39b676731e34" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a9a38a9f-1171-55bf-a06a-39b676731e34" :step/id #uuid "a9a38a9f-1171-55bf-a06a-39b676731e34" :step/content 17592186266053 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186053961 :answer-field/choices 17592186053962] [:db/retract 17592186053961 :answer-field/choices 17592186053963] [:db/retract 17592186053961 :answer-field/choices 17592186053964] [:db/retract 17592186053961 :answer-field/choices 17592186053965] [:db/retract 17592186053961 :answer-field/choices 17592186053966] {:db/id 17592186054618 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7b28b38e-826f-526f-9fc1-5f33d99b85c5" "question-43125a76-2814-5690-9f8b-34c658dd4ad3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054529 :question/problem "Evaluate $(4 + 3\\,\\textrm{i}) + (1 + 5\\,\\textrm{i}).$" :question/worked-solution "To add complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+3\\,\\text{i})+(1+5\\,\\text{i}) & =(4+3\\,\\text{i})+(1+5\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(4+1)+(3+5)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =5+8\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054619 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8e5bfd0-3749-5d98-b80f-2065e3b9d3d1" "question-ef858a39-0a28-52af-9941-ce2f2a8713ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054530 :question/problem "Find the value of $(5 + 4\\,\\textrm{i}) - (1 + 2\\,\\textrm{i}).$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+4\\,\\text{i})-(1+2\\,\\text{i}) & =(5+4\\,\\text{i})-(1+2\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5-1)+(4-2)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =4+2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054620 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-683bbf9d-fe44-596b-9cba-6b07323bd6d5" "question-40969b77-0e08-50d1-86b5-46f58976876a" "question-bbd97f04-776a-53ed-8d9f-1d2d316080bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054531 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\sqrt{-9} \\right) + (3 - \\sqrt{-16}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the two complex numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{-9})+(3-\\sqrt{-16}) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3\\,\\text{i})+(3-4\\,\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo add these two complex numbers, we just add the real parts and add the imaginary parts separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}+3\\,\\text{i})+(3-4\\,\\text{i}) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3)+(3+(-4))\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+3)+(3-4)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-1\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054621 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-246ccde2-7d9b-5b08-9b39-f9d20f01227f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054532 :question/problem "Find the value of $5\\,\\textrm{i} - 8\\,\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract two complex numbers, we just subtract the real parts and subtract the imaginary parts separately.\n\nIn this case, $5\\,\\textrm{i}$ and $8\\,\\textrm{i}$ are purely imaginary numbers. Therefore, we only need to subtract their imaginary parts, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,\\text{i}-8\\,\\text{i}=(5-8)\\,\\text{i} & =-3\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b28b38e-826f-526f-9fc1-5f33d99b85c5" :question/id #uuid "7b28b38e-826f-526f-9fc1-5f33d99b85c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112858" :question/problem "$(7+10\\,\\text{i})+(5+3\\,\\text{i})=$" :question/worked-solution "Given the complex number $a+b\\,\\text{i},$ $a$ represents its real part while $b$ represents its imaginary part.\n\nTo add or subtract complex numbers, we add or subtract their real parts, and we add or subtract their imaginary parts.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7+10\\,\\text{i})+(5+3\\,\\text{i}) & =(7+5)+(10+3)\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =12+13\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45188644-8b02-5716-ad9f-533d5c1afabc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45188644-8b02-5716-ad9f-533d5c1afabc" :answer-field/id #uuid "45188644-8b02-5716-ad9f-53