rt by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42f6efb1-acc1-594f-9863-9843b604f60e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42f6efb1-acc1-594f-9863-9843b604f60e" :answer-field/id #uuid "42f6efb1-acc1-594f-9863-9843b604f60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dec7850d-5fd9-5c84-b294-33569ce40c41" "answer-09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" "answer-81cf656b-bd3c-5484-968b-6e7bc93c98ad" "answer-b4fe22da-80f5-5f8c-9080-2147a668ed55" "answer-4f2855e0-fb0a-5301-b062-4ac8a822906c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec7850d-5fd9-5c84-b294-33569ce40c41" :answer/id #uuid "dec7850d-5fd9-5c84-b294-33569ce40c41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{1/2}}{3}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer/id #uuid "09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81cf656b-bd3c-5484-968b-6e7bc93c98ad" :answer/id #uuid "81cf656b-bd3c-5484-968b-6e7bc93c98ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4fe22da-80f5-5f8c-9080-2147a668ed55" :answer/id #uuid "b4fe22da-80f5-5f8c-9080-2147a668ed55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{1/2}}{2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f2855e0-fb0a-5301-b062-4ac8a822906c" :answer/id #uuid "4f2855e0-fb0a-5301-b062-4ac8a822906c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" :question/id #uuid "37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-29665" :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15bc8d9e-bde6-57af-844a-0b82646dbe35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15bc8d9e-bde6-57af-844a-0b82646dbe35" :answer-field/id #uuid "15bc8d9e-bde6-57af-844a-0b82646dbe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-943d70e7-e52b-597e-9871-44857a8a7ae4" "answer-877168ac-4c34-57ba-a846-5362369f5757" "answer-c6b24cd8-5808-5ff4-8dd5-6d0e70ccaa9e" "answer-e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" "answer-e25dd75d-4458-58ea-a630-3ccbc3fecb7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-943d70e7-e52b-597e-9871-44857a8a7ae4" :answer/id #uuid "943d70e7-e52b-597e-9871-44857a8a7ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-877168ac-4c34-57ba-a846-5362369f5757" :answer/id #uuid "877168ac-4c34-57ba-a846-5362369f5757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6b24cd8-5808-5ff4-8dd5-6d0e70ccaa9e" :answer/id #uuid "c6b24cd8-5808-5ff4-8dd5-6d0e70ccaa9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer/id #uuid "e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e25dd75d-4458-58ea-a630-3ccbc3fecb7b" :answer/id #uuid "e25dd75d-4458-58ea-a630-3ccbc3fecb7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" :question/id #uuid "3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-113703" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a255f30f-2d4d-5a18-8010-7066f9da2d12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a255f30f-2d4d-5a18-8010-7066f9da2d12" :answer-field/id #uuid "a255f30f-2d4d-5a18-8010-7066f9da2d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46f4c2a1-30c4-58bc-a872-acf707c7f214" "answer-25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" "answer-fd7a9df8-4ded-54ff-9dd7-df5316a89579" "answer-bf5da9ff-937c-53a4-b1fa-cc6b757fbfad" "answer-dae23439-686a-536f-8883-85442f6a6ebc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46f4c2a1-30c4-58bc-a872-acf707c7f214" :answer/id #uuid "46f4c2a1-30c4-58bc-a872-acf707c7f214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer/id #uuid "25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd7a9df8-4ded-54ff-9dd7-df5316a89579" :answer/id #uuid "fd7a9df8-4ded-54ff-9dd7-df5316a89579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2{x}^{5/2}}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf5da9ff-937c-53a4-b1fa-cc6b757fbfad" :answer/id #uuid "bf5da9ff-937c-53a4-b1fa-cc6b757fbfad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae23439-686a-536f-8883-85442f6a6ebc" :answer/id #uuid "dae23439-686a-536f-8883-85442f6a6ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234299 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" :answer-field/id #uuid "f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" "answer-979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" "answer-47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" "answer-21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" "answer-3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" :answer/id #uuid "b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" :answer/id #uuid "979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{5/7}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/id #uuid "47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{1/7}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/id #uuid "21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" :question/id #uuid "28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-29993" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eae9e030-db6a-548d-840a-748c2245c7ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eae9e030-db6a-548d-840a-748c2245c7ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "eae9e030-db6a-548d-840a-748c2245c7ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" "answer-06eef846-65a9-5990-acd0-b045e13d1cf7" "answer-b0ca349d-23a9-53ce-9e6b-be88f7c8ec44" "answer-393f1a37-77c6-5bbd-a580-db9adc219b01" "answer-c14ac74e-1927-56c7-a26e-5732f3c912ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer/id #uuid "68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{x}^{-1/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06eef846-65a9-5990-acd0-b045e13d1cf7" :answer/id #uuid "06eef846-65a9-5990-acd0-b045e13d1cf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0ca349d-23a9-53ce-9e6b-be88f7c8ec44" :answer/id #uuid "b0ca349d-23a9-53ce-9e6b-be88f7c8ec44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-393f1a37-77c6-5bbd-a580-db9adc219b01" :answer/id #uuid "393f1a37-77c6-5bbd-a580-db9adc219b01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{-1/3}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14ac74e-1927-56c7-a26e-5732f3c912ca" :answer/id #uuid "c14ac74e-1927-56c7-a26e-5732f3c912ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{-3/2}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065405 :tutorial/content "An **antiderivative** is the *opposite* of a derivative. For example:\n\n- Using the power rule, we know that the *derivative* of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x}.$\n\n- Going in the *opposite* direction, we say that an **antiderivative** of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\nFor any given function, there are infinitely many antiderivatives. For example:\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so an *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+1$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+1}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+2}.$\n\n- ...\n\nIn general, all the antiderivatives of $\\color{red}2x$ can be written in the form ${\\color{blue}x^2+C},$ where $C$ is a constant.\n\nTo represent the antiderivative symbolically, we use a special symbol $\\displaystyle \\int$ called the **integral** symbol. So, using **integral notation**, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x = x^2 + C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ represents an arbitrary constant and is called the **constant of integration**.\n\nIn words, $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x$ is said as “the integral of $2x$ with respect to $x$” or “the antiderivative of $2x$.” Integrals like this are called **indefinite integrals**, and the function being **integrated** ($2x$ in this case) is called the **integrand**." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065406 :tutorial/content "Let's recap the main points:\n\n- Integrals like $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,\\textrm d x$ are called **indefinite integrals**.\n\n- Whenever we calculate an indefinite integral, we must always remember to add an **arbitrary constant** $+C.$\n\n- To integrate power functions, we can use the **power rule for integration**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C,\n$$\n\nprovided that $n \\neq -1.$ To emphasize, the power rule does *not* work when $n=-1.$ So we cannot use the above formula to work out $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$ Integrals like this need to be handled differently, and you'll find out how soon." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064406 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064258 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1169d5be-77b9-5c01-b29e-d873231e9f86"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1169d5be-77b9-5c01-b29e-d873231e9f86" :question/id #uuid "1169d5be-77b9-5c01-b29e-d873231e9f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-2591" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6b}{3}+\\frac{9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6}{3}\\cdot b+\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot c & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-2b+3c & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33f1ff95-b84b-5146-8e81-b9dec5eae7f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33f1ff95-b84b-5146-8e81-b9dec5eae7f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "33f1ff95-b84b-5146-8e81-b9dec5eae7f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-000d1ee2-eb0e-516d-880b-59e76d658afd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2165713-727d-5608-af21-2766b713333b" "answer-000d1ee2-eb0e-516d-880b-59e76d658afd" "answer-3def3569-bf02-567b-a7ce-c0676ebb8e33" "answer-a1c1327e-3c63-5535-94ec-15c702bd94af" "answer-17d560ac-9cc4-58ec-bd8a-4cc87ded49ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2165713-727d-5608-af21-2766b713333b" :answer/id #uuid "d2165713-727d-5608-af21-2766b713333b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}+2b-3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-000d1ee2-eb0e-516d-880b-59e76d658afd" :answer/id #uuid "000d1ee2-eb0e-516d-880b-59e76d658afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}-2b+3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3def3569-bf02-567b-a7ce-c0676ebb8e33" :answer/id #uuid "3def3569-bf02-567b-a7ce-c0676ebb8e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}-2b-3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c1327e-3c63-5535-94ec-15c702bd94af" :answer/id #uuid "a1c1327e-3c63-5535-94ec-15c702bd94af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7abc}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17d560ac-9cc4-58ec-bd8a-4cc87ded49ab" :answer/id #uuid "17d560ac-9cc4-58ec-bd8a-4cc87ded49ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-2b+3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064257 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-69e15305-fd39-5e12-9afd-c74d993158ed" "question-b8beba6a-7b91-5602-93f9-62eba4e754b8" "question-18e51362-5c55-557f-b76e-006c8ec5e5f2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-69e15305-fd39-5e12-9afd-c74d993158ed" :question/id #uuid "69e15305-fd39-5e12-9afd-c74d993158ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-2593" :question/problem "If $\\frac{8x-12y}{4}$ is written in the form $ax+by,$ what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8x}{4}-\\frac{12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4}\\cdot x-\\frac{12}{4}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2x-3y & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{b}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-23253a0c-91f5-5d73-8605-1ae876bdd4b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23253a0c-91f5-5d73-8605-1ae876bdd4b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "23253a0c-91f5-5d73-8605-1ae876bdd4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75c969ca-367d-5068-acbe-f0467f813227" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8331f74-2394-5f6e-af1d-324225a8b66a" "answer-75c969ca-367d-5068-acbe-f0467f813227" "answer-c1682820-b145-57af-bb60-67f57aa12777" "answer-e31a9be0-36c9-524a-9bf7-ae092f66b308" "answer-cd8541b1-df31-5bf9-9595-f8be69351a38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8331f74-2394-5f6e-af1d-324225a8b66a" :answer/id #uuid "f8331f74-2394-5f6e-af1d-324225a8b66a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=-12$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c969ca-367d-5068-acbe-f0467f813227" :answer/id #uuid "75c969ca-367d-5068-acbe-f0467f813227" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=-3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1682820-b145-57af-bb60-67f57aa12777" :answer/id #uuid "c1682820-b145-57af-bb60-67f57aa12777" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e31a9be0-36c9-524a-9bf7-ae092f66b308" :answer/id